s are compared in Figure 2 assuming vehicle speed v=3km/h on 4GHz, 120km/h on 4GHz, and 3km/h on 30GHz, respectively. Detailed simulation assumption is in Appendix A. We can see that the NR-DMRS/TSS is beneficial to improve the channel estimation in case of low mobility, e.g., v=3km/h and also make PBCH performance robustness against Doppler spreading in case of high mobility, e.g., in v=120km/h. Proposal 3: Together with NR-SSS, the sequence-based NR-DMRS/TSS should be considered to improve the channel estimation of NR-PBCH demodulation. Transmission schemes and the number of antenna ports We assume the NR-SSS has same number of antenna port(s) as NR-PBCH and the NR-SSS is sent on the same antenna port as that of NR-PBCH. Assuming same tx power and 2x2 antenna configuration, different transmit diversity are compared based on simulation. Alt.1: Two antenna port based SFBC Alt.2: Single antenna port based transmission scheme In Atl1, the channel estimation uses the NR-SSS+NR-DMRS/TSS designed for 2 antenna ports. But in Alt2, the channel estimation uses the NR-SSS+NR-DMRS/TSS designed for 1 antenna port. The use of tx diversity schemes is transparent to the UEs, which only observes single radio link with equivalent combined channel. In the simulation, we apply precoder cycling across SSS and PBCH symbols within each radio frames and change the precoder cycling parameters over multiple radio frames, such as [1 1]T, [1 -1]T, [1 j]T, [1 –j]T. The different tx diversity schemes are compared in Figure 3-(a) under channel delay spread of 100ns and 1000ns on 4GHz, and in Figure 3-(b) under channel delay spread of 100ns on 30GHz frequency, respectively. The SISO performance is also given as a reference. The other detailed simulation assumption is given in Appendix A. In Figure 3, we use the IFDM o

